    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d784">

                <pb facs="0634" n="626" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg626"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.51/6525: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Hong Kong</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Southard</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hong
                                Kong</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-12-02T09:00:00+08:00">December 2, 1937—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 2—7:20 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Referring to my telegram of October 18,4 p.m., shipments of Chinese silver from
                    Hong Kong to London are continuing as reported in <pb facs="0635" n="627"
                        xml:id="pg627"/> the telegram under reference and it is reliably estimated
                    that all stocks of such silver will have left Hong Kong by January 15th.</p>
                <p>During the past 6 weeks Chinese Government silver to the value of approximately
                    20 million Hong Kong dollars has arrived in Hong Kong.</p>
                <p>Repeated to the Ambassador at Hankow.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Southard</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
